{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310554,"nonce":"0+G7SHNqW7mtivcF0xAQH3xRhAc=","title":"FIXED b64a0083 (issue 89af5b5): the recurring '1 less in CI' row was Issue3635: HEAP CORRUPTION that glibc detects (malloc_consolidate) and ...","message":"**THE SYMPTOM:** CI (Linux, `.github/workflows/conformance.yml`) scored\nexactly ONE lower than local for weeks: Issue3635 PASS locally, CRASH in CI\nwith `malloc_consolidate(): invalid chunk size` then SIGABRT. Diff the two\nreports with the artifact: `gh run download \u003cid\u003e -n conformance-results`.\n\n**THE MECHANISM (two defects, 2026-09-12):**\n1. `dB.key = { x: 10 }` with `dB:Dynamic\u003cB\u003e` — the field WRITE path\n   (mir/field/lvalue.rs) resolved `key` BY NAME across every class\n   (`resolve_field_index_by_name`) and emitted a fixed-slot store\n   (`gep [3]`) into an anonymous object with NO such slot: a write past the\n   allocation. glibc's allocator notices at the next consolidate; macOS's\n   never does. Fix: a Dynamic receiver never takes the slot path — it goes\n   to the reflective write (`haxe_reflect_set_field`) and the value is\n   boxed for it (the Assign's LHS target type is Dynamic for a field of a\n   Dynamic).\n2. `dB = {}` — the parser reads empty braces as an empty BLOCK, which\n   lowers to NULL. In a VALUE position whose expected type is Dynamic or\n   anonymous, `{}` is Haxe's empty object declaration: the typer now\n   rewrites it (`expects_object_value`, from expected_arg_type_stack /\n   expected_new_type_hint). Function bodies and `if (c) {}` stay blocks.\n\n**HOW TO SEE IT ON MACOS:** `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=/usr/lib/libgmalloc.dylib\nrayzor run ...` — guard malloc puts every allocation at a page end, so an\nout-of-bounds write SEGFAULTS instead of corrupting silently. The full test\ncrashed under it at once; statement-level bisection of the instance method\n(variants written into a copy of the conformance case dir) found the line.\n`MallocScribble`/`MallocGuardEdges` did NOT catch it.\n\n**RULE:** a row that passes on macOS and crashes on Linux with a malloc\nmessage is memory corruption, not a Linux bug. Reach for libgmalloc first,\nor `ssh nuc` (never a local VM) with the build recipe in\n`~/ash_ab_build.sh` (`LLVM_SYS_211_PREFIX=/usr/lib/llvm-22`,\n`LIBCLANG_PATH=/usr/lib/llvm-21/lib`, `RUSTFLAGS=\"-L native=$HOME/linkfix\"`).\n\n**LANDED b64a0083 (git-bug 89af5b5), 533 -\u003e 536, LOST 0, suite 207/0/1.**\nGoing reflective exposed a THIRD defect: a property of an ABSTRACT\n(Issue7674 `abstractProperty`) had been resolved through the same by-name\nslot. It has no slot; it reads/writes through its accessor methods\n(`abstract_property_accessors`, keyed by the property SYMBOL). Writes\nconsult it FIRST, reads LAST — the symbol is SHARED with a same-named field\nof the underlying class (`Int64.high` over `__Int64.high`), whose slot must\nkeep winning; registering it in `property_access_map` broke Int64.\nTRAP: register the value's HIR type for the reflective write\n(`pending_store_value_ty`) — a handle and a box share a register type, and\nboxing on the Assign side stored a box into a CLASS slot (test_derive_clone).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_ci_only_heap_corruption_issue3635` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}